<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zheng Zhilong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1604">1604</a>–<a href="/years/1661">1661</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Nicholas Iquan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zheng Zhilong was a Chinese merchant and pirate who built a great maritime trading network from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. He entered Ming service as an admiral, supported the Ming loyalist court in Fujian, then surrendered to the Qing in 1646. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a> continued the resistance, and the Qing executed Zheng Zhilong in 1661.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zheng Zhilong was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, the son of Zheng Shaozu, a mid-level financial official, and Lady Huang. He left home as a teenager on a merchant ship, either because he wanted to or because his father threw him out for constant fighting, and went to his uncle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, where he was baptised a Catholic as Nicholas Gaspard. Sent with cargo to Hirado in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, he met Li Dan, the city's Kapitan Cina, who found him work as an interpreter for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. In Japan he married Tagawa Matsu, who bore their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a> in 1624.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1624 Zheng became a privateer for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, and after Li Dan's death in 1625 he took over his fleet and led the Chinese pirates unopposed. In 1625 he founded the Shibazhi, a band of eighteen pirate leaders. By 1627 he commanded four hundred junks and tens of thousands of men, and he sold protection passes to fishermen and merchants. In 1628 he defeated the Ming fleet, then switched to official service and was made a major general.</p><p>As Admiral of the Coastal Seas he defeated a force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ships and junks under the renegade Liu Xiang at the Battle of Liaoluo Bay on 22 October 1633. The spoils let him buy as much as 60% of the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, and his annual income was later estimated at three to four times that of the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1644 he stayed loyal to the Ming, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> was captured in 1645 he was ordered to defend the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> under the Prince of Tang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When he defected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> in 1646 he left the passes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> unguarded, and Qing forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>. His brothers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a> refused to follow him, and seeing that his army had not come over, the Qing placed him under house arrest and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>; his bodyguard of former African slaves died trying to stop the arrest. Accounts differ on whether Tagawa Matsu was raped and then killed herself, or died directing the fight against the Qing at Anhai. Because Koxinga kept up his resistance, Zheng was beheaded at the Caishikou execution grounds in Beijing in 1661.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zheng Zhilong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Koxinga (Zheng Chenggong), son of the merchant-admiral <b>Zheng Zhilong</b>, led Ming loyalist forces on the Fujian coast after 1646.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tungning">Kingdom of Tungning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nan'an, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.96,118.38&amp;year=1641" aria-label="Nan'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7691 1245 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zheng Zhilong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8141 1515h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8141 1515h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.96,118.38&amp;year=1641">What was here in 1641? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
